    "case_id": "156",
    "case_summary": "A 66-year-old woman with a history of endometrioid endometrial carcinoma presented with abdominal and pelvic pain. Physical examination was unremarkable, and laboratory studies, including a creatinine level of 0.81 mg dl–1, were within normal limits. Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis demonstrated a right renal mass with areas of fat attenuation located in the upper pole, creating a defect in the renal parenchyma. The lesion contained visible vessels extending into the renal cortex, a finding known as a “renal parenchymal vascular pedicle.” Additionally, there was a fatty tumor thrombus within the right renal vein extending to its confluence with the inferior vena cava. No other masses or enlarged lymph nodes were identified.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Angiomyolipoma\nB. perirenal liposarcoma\nC. renal cell carcinoma\nD. renal oncocytoma\nE. perirenal liposarcoma typically reveals a renal hilar vascular pedicle, with tumour vessels extending from the fatty\nF. on the basis of … presence of calcifications; large, irregular tumour invading the perirenal or sinus fat; large\nG. None\nH. None\n",
